  ('Economic growth', 'an increase in the real output (real GDP) of an economy over time.', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Nominal GDP', 'GDP measured in current prices, not adjusted for inflation.', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Real GDP', 'GDP adjusted for inflation, reflecting the actual volume of output.', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Unemployment', 'a situation where people who are willing and able to work, and are actively seeking work, cannot find a job.', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Unemployment rate', 'the percentage of the labour force that is unemployed.', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Frictional unemployment', 'short-term unemployment that occurs when people are between jobs.', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Structural unemployment', 'unemployment caused by a mismatch of skills/location due to changes in the structure of the economy (decline of an industry).', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Cyclical (demand-deficient) unemployment', 'unemployment caused by a fall in aggregate demand during a downturn/recession.', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Seasonal unemployment', 'unemployment caused by seasonal changes in demand for labour.', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Technological unemployment', 'unemployment caused by workers being replaced by machinery/automation.', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Inflation', 'a sustained/persistent rise in the general price level of an economy over time.', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Deflation', 'a sustained/persistent fall in the general price level.', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Disinflation', 'a fall in the rate of inflation (prices still rising, but more slowly).', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Consumer Price Index (CPI)', 'a measure of the average change in prices of a representative basket of goods and services purchased by households.', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Nominal values', 'values measured in current, money terms.', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Real values', 'values adjusted for inflation.', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Cost-push inflation', 'inflation caused by a rise in production costs (e.g. wages, raw materials), shifting AS left.', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Demand-pull inflation', 'inflation caused by excessive aggregate demand relative to aggregate supply.', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Macroeconomic policy objectives', 'government goals typically including price stability, low unemployment and economic growth.', 'The macroeconomy: measures and performance'),
  ('Fiscal policy', 'the use of government spending and taxation to influence aggregate demand and achieve macroeconomic objectives.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Government budget', 'a statement of planned government spending and revenue (mainly taxation) over a period.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Budget deficit', 'a situation where government spending exceeds government revenue.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Budget surplus', 'a situation where government revenue exceeds government spending.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('National debt', 'the total accumulated amount owed by the government as a result of past budget deficits.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Direct tax', 'a tax levied directly on income or wealth, paid directly to the government by the taxpayer (e.g. income tax).', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Indirect tax', 'a tax on spending/goods and services.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Progressive tax', 'average tax rate rises as income rises.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Regressive tax', 'average tax rate falls as income rises.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Proportional tax', 'average tax rate stays constant as income changes.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Marginal rate of tax (mrt)', 'the proportion of an additional unit of income paid in tax.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Average rate of tax (art)', 'total tax paid divided by total income.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Capital spending (investment spending)', 'government spending on long-term assets/infrastructure.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Current spending', 'government spending on day-to-day items (e.g. wages, supplies).', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Expansionary fiscal policy', 'increased government spending and/or reduced taxation to raise AD.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Contractionary fiscal policy', 'reduced government spending and/or increased taxation to reduce AD.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Monetary policy', 'the use of interest rates, money supply and credit regulations by the central bank/government to influence AD and macroeconomic objectives.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Interest rate', 'the cost of borrowing money or the reward for saving money, expressed as a percentage.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Money supply', 'the total stock of money circulating in an economy.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Credit regulations', 'rules governing the availability and terms of borrowing/lending.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Expansionary monetary policy', 'lower interest rates and/or increased money supply to raise AD.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Contractionary monetary policy', 'higher interest rates and/or reduced money supply to reduce AD.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Supply-side policy', 'government policy aimed at increasing the productive capacity/potential output of an economy by shifting LRAS to the right.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Productivity', 'output per unit of input (e.g. output per worker).', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Productive capacity', 'the maximum output an economy can produce with existing resources.', 'Macroeconomic policy'),
  ('Absolute advantage', 'the ability of a country to produce a good using fewer resources (more efficiently) than another country.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Comparative advantage', 'the ability of a country to produce a good at a lower opportunity cost than another country.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Specialisation (international)', 'countries concentrating on producing goods in which they have a comparative advantage.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Free trade', 'trade between countries without any government-imposed restrictions.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Trade liberalisation', 'the process of reducing barriers to international trade.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Trading possibility curve', 'a curve showing the combinations of goods a country can consume after specialisation and trade, lying outside its PPC.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Terms of trade', 'the ratio of a country''s average export prices to its average import prices, indicating the rate at which exports exchange for imports.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Protectionism', 'government policies restricting free trade to protect domestic industries.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Tariff', 'a tax imposed on imported goods.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Import quota', 'a physical limit on the quantity of a good that can be imported.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Export subsidy', 'a payment by government to domestic producers to encourage exports.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Embargo', 'a complete ban on trade (imports or exports) with a particular country or of a particular good.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Administrative burden ("red tape")', 'bureaucratic/regulatory requirements that make importing more difficult or costly.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Balance of payments', 'a record of all financial transactions between a country''s residents and the rest of the world.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Current account', 'the section of the balance of payments recording trade in goods, trade in services, primary income and secondary income.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Trade in goods', 'exports minus imports of physical/tangible products.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Trade in services', 'exports minus imports of intangible services (e.g. tourism, finance).', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Primary income', 'income flows from investment (interest, profits, dividends) and employment abroad.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Secondary income', 'transfers such as remittances, foreign aid and grants with no good/service exchanged.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Current account deficit', 'occurs when outflows on the current account exceed inflows.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Current account surplus', 'occurs when inflows on the current account exceed outflows.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Exchange rate', 'the price of one currency expressed in terms of another currency.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Floating exchange rate', 'an exchange rate determined solely by the forces of demand and supply for a currency, with no government intervention.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Depreciation', 'a fall in the value of a floating currency against other currencies.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Appreciation', 'a rise in the value of a floating currency against other currencies.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Expenditure-switching policy', 'policy aimed at shifting demand between domestic and foreign goods (e.g. tariffs, depreciation).', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Expenditure-reducing policy', 'policy aimed at reducing overall spending in the economy (e.g. contractionary fiscal/monetary policy) to reduce imports.', 'International trade and exchange rates'),
  ('Utility', 'the satisfaction/benefit a consumer derives from consuming a good or service.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Total utility (TU)', 'the total satisfaction gained from consuming a given quantity of a good.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Marginal utility (MU)', 'the additional satisfaction gained from consuming one more unit of a good.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Law of diminishing marginal utility', 'as more units of a good are consumed, the additional (marginal) utility from each extra unit eventually declines.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Equi-marginal principle', 'consumers maximise total utility by allocating spending so that the marginal utility per dollar spent is equal across all goods.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Indifference curve', 'a curve showing all combinations of two goods that give a consumer the same level of total utility/satisfaction.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Budget line', 'a line showing all combinations of two goods a consumer can afford, given income and prices.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Income effect', 'the change in quantity demanded resulting from a change in real income caused by a price change.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Substitution effect', 'the change in quantity demanded resulting from a change in the relative price of a good compared to substitutes.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Giffen good', 'an inferior good for which the income effect outweighs the substitution effect, so quantity demanded rises as price rises (upward-sloping demand curve).', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Productive efficiency', 'occurs when output is produced at the lowest possible cost (on the production possibility curve / at the minimum point of the average cost curve).', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Allocative efficiency', 'occurs when resources are allocated so that price equals marginal cost (P = MC), reflecting consumer preferences.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Pareto optimality', 'a situation where resources are allocated such that no one can be made better off without making someone else worse off.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Dynamic efficiency', 'efficiency achieved over time through innovation and investment, improving products/processes.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Market failure', 'occurs when the free market fails to allocate resources efficiently, resulting in a net welfare loss to society.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
