  ('Social benefit', 'the total benefit of an activity to society = private benefit + external benefit.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Externality', 'a spillover effect (cost or benefit) of production/consumption affecting third parties, not reflected in market prices.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Positive externality', 'a beneficial spillover effect on third parties (e.g. education).', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Negative externality', 'a harmful spillover effect on third parties (e.g. pollution).', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Deadweight welfare loss', 'the net loss of economic welfare to society caused by market failure (e.g. resulting from externalities).', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Asymmetric information', 'a situation where one party to a transaction has more/better information than the other.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Moral hazard', 'a situation where one party takes on more risk because they do not bear the full costs of that risk (often due to insurance or asymmetric information).', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Fixed factors of production', 'inputs that cannot be changed in the short run.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Variable factors of production', 'inputs that can be changed in the short run.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Total product', 'the total output produced by a firm using a given quantity of factors.', 'Consumer choice, efficiency and market failure'),
  ('Average product', 'total product divided by the quantity of the variable factor employed.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Marginal product', 'the additional output produced by employing one more unit of the variable factor.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Law of diminishing returns (law of variable proportions)', 'as more units of a variable factor are added to a fixed factor, the marginal product of the variable factor will eventually decline.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Fixed costs (FC)', 'costs that do not vary with output in the short run.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Variable costs (VC)', 'costs that vary directly with the level of output.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Total cost (TC)', 'the sum of fixed costs and variable costs (TC = FC + VC).', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Average cost (AC)', 'total cost divided by output (AC = TC/Q).', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Marginal cost (MC)', 'the additional cost of producing one more unit of output.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Average total cost (ATC)', 'total cost per unit of output.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Average fixed cost (AFC)', 'fixed cost per unit of output.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Average variable cost (AVC)', 'variable cost per unit of output.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Returns to scale', 'the proportionate change in output resulting from a proportionate change in all factor inputs in the long run.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Long-run average cost (LRAC) curve', 'shows the lowest average cost of producing each level of output when all factors are variable.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Minimum efficient scale (MES)', 'the lowest level of output at which a firm can minimise its long-run average costs.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Economies of scale', 'factors causing average cost to fall as output increases in the long run.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Internal economies of scale', 'cost savings arising within a firm as it grows (e.g. technical, managerial, financial, marketing, purchasing/bulk-buying, risk-bearing).', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('External economies of scale', 'cost savings arising from the growth of the whole industry, benefiting all firms within it.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Diseconomies of scale', 'factors causing average cost to rise as output increases beyond a certain point.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Internal diseconomies of scale', 'rising average costs arising within a firm as it grows too large (e.g. management/coordination problems).', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('External diseconomies of scale', 'rising average costs arising from the growth of the whole industry (e.g. congestion, competition for resources).', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Total revenue (TR)', 'total income received from sales (price × quantity).', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Average revenue (AR)', 'revenue per unit sold (equals price for a single-price firm).', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Marginal revenue (MR)', 'the additional revenue from selling one more unit of output.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Normal profit', 'the minimum level of profit needed to keep a firm/entrepreneur in its current line of business (treated as a cost of production).', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Subnormal profit', 'profit below normal profit (a loss situation in economic terms).', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Supernormal (abnormal) profit', 'profit in excess of normal profit.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Perfect competition', 'a market structure with many buyers/sellers, homogeneous products, free entry/exit and perfect information.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Imperfect competition', 'any market structure that does not meet the conditions of perfect competition.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Monopoly', 'a market structure with a single seller/dominant firm and significant barriers to entry.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Monopolistic competition', 'a market structure with many firms, differentiated products and relatively free entry/exit.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Oligopoly', 'a market structure dominated by a few large firms, often with interdependent decision-making.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Natural monopoly', 'a market where economies of scale are so significant that a single firm can supply the entire market at a lower cost than multiple firms.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Barriers to entry', 'obstacles preventing new firms from entering a market (legal, market, cost, physical).', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Legal barriers', 'barriers such as patents, licences and copyrights that restrict entry.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Market barriers', 'barriers such as brand loyalty and control of distribution channels.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Cost barriers', 'barriers such as high sunk/start-up costs or economies of scale enjoyed by incumbents.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Physical barriers', 'barriers such as limited availability of essential resources or space.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Shutdown price', 'the price below which a firm will stop producing because revenue does not cover variable costs (short run) or total costs (long run).', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Contestable market', 'a market with low barriers to entry and exit (including low sunk costs), where the threat of new entrants disciplines incumbent firms.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Price competition', 'firms competing by altering price.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Non-price competition', 'firms competing through means other than price (e.g. advertising, branding, quality).', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Collusion', 'an agreement between firms to coordinate behaviour (e.g. fix prices or output) to reduce competition.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Prisoner''s Dilemma', 'a game theory model showing that individual firms may rationally choose not to cooperate (collude) even though cooperation would benefit both, due to mutual distrust.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Concentration ratio', 'the proportion of total market output/sales accounted for by a given number (e.g. top 4 or top 5) of the largest firms in an industry.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Internal (organic) growth', 'growth achieved by expanding a firm''s own output/capacity internally.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Diversification', 'expanding into new markets or products, often to spread risk.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('External growth', 'growth achieved through merging with or acquiring other firms.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Merger/takeover (integration)', 'the combination of two or more firms into one.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Horizontal integration', 'merger between firms at the same stage of production in the same industry.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Vertical integration', 'merger between firms at different stages of production in the same industry.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Forward vertical integration', 'merger with a firm closer to the final consumer.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Backward vertical integration', 'merger with a firm closer to the source of raw materials.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Conglomerate integration', 'merger between firms in unrelated industries.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Cartel', 'a formal agreement among firms in an industry to collude on price and/or output to maximise joint profits.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Principal–agent problem', 'a conflict of interest arising when the objectives of owners/shareholders (principals) differ from those of managers (agents) who run the firm.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Profit maximisation', 'the traditional objective of firms, achieved where marginal revenue equals marginal cost (MR = MC).', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Survival', 'a firm''s objective of remaining in business, often prioritised during difficult trading conditions.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Profit satisficing', 'aiming for an acceptable, rather than maximum, level of profit.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Sales maximisation', 'aiming to maximise total revenue rather than profit.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Revenue maximisation', 'producing at the output where marginal revenue equals zero.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Price discrimination', 'charging different prices to different consumers/groups for the same good, not justified by cost differences.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('First-degree price discrimination', 'charging each consumer the maximum price they are willing to pay.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Second-degree price discrimination', 'charging different prices based on the quantity consumed (e.g. bulk discounts).', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Third-degree price discrimination', 'charging different prices to different identifiable groups/markets.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Limit pricing', 'setting a low price to deter potential new entrants from entering the market.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Predatory pricing', 'deliberately setting a very low (or below-cost) price to force rivals out of the market.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Price leadership', 'a pricing pattern where one dominant firm sets the price and other firms follow.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Kinked demand curve', 'a model of oligopoly demand showing price rigidity, based on the assumption rivals follow price cuts but not price rises.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Ad valorem tax', 'a tax charged as a percentage of the price of a good.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Production quota', 'a government-imposed limit on the quantity of a good that can be produced.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Prohibition', 'a complete legal ban on the production/sale/consumption of a good.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Licence', 'legal permission required to produce, sell or consume certain goods, used to restrict market activity.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Regulation', 'rules imposed by government to control the behaviour of firms/individuals in a market.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Deregulation', 'the removal or reduction of government rules/regulations in a market.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Pollution permit', 'a tradeable licence allowing a firm to emit a certain amount of pollution, used to control negative externalities.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Property rights', 'legal rights of ownership that allow the private allocation and enforcement of resource use, used to internalise externalities.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Nationalisation', 'transfer of ownership of firms/industries from the private sector to the government.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Privatisation', 'transfer of ownership of firms/industries from the government to the private sector.', 'Costs, revenue and market structures'),
  ('Behavioural insights/"nudge" theory', 'using an understanding of psychological biases to design policies that encourage (without forcing) people to make certain choices.', 'Labour markets and income distribution'),
  ('Government failure', 'a situation where government intervention in a market leads to a net welfare loss, i.e. a misallocation of resources worse than before intervention.', 'Labour markets and income distribution'),
  ('Equity', 'fairness in the distribution of income/wealth/opportunities (a normative concept).', 'Labour markets and income distribution'),
  ('Equality', 'an identical/equal distribution of income, wealth or opportunities.', 'Labour markets and income distribution'),
  ('Absolute poverty', 'a situation where individuals lack the minimum income/resources needed to meet basic needs.', 'Labour markets and income distribution'),
  ('Relative poverty', 'a situation where individuals have significantly less income/wealth than the average in their society.', 'Labour markets and income distribution'),
